                <pb facs="0344" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg340"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1643: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lochhart</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-06-04T16:00:00-04:00">June 4, 1938—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>361. Your 771, June 3, 8 p.m. The Department desires that you inform the Japanese
                    Consul General that this Government cannot admit the right of the Japanese
                    authorities to enforce such regulations <pb facs="0345" n="341" xml:id="pg341"/>
                    against Americans. You may add that you desire, however, to cooperate in
                    reasonable measures for the prevention of the spread of cholera and that you
                    will recommend to Americans that they voluntarily provide themselves with
                    cholera certificates or identity papers for presentation to the health
                    authorities when requested. You may also include in your statement your proposed
                    caution in regard to the possibility of incidents arising.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
